{"duration_api_ms":134190,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"dc22f639-281a-4b51-9252-db96131caef2","total_cost_usd":0.2239116,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10245,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"output_tokens":18202,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":9950},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10245,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":18202,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10245,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10245},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":18202,"cacheReadInputTokens":4538,"cacheCreationInputTokens":10245,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.2239116,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":9950,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\\n\\nEvery credential has a cap. When that cap is spent the gateway answers 429 at the edge — without waking the service behind it — so the refusal costs the platform almost nothing and costs a caller that files it under transport error a great deal. An earlier draft of this note argued for a client-side token bucket that mirrored the gateway's own counters; that section has been left out on purpose, because two counters that disagree are worse than one counter that refuses, which is the whole reason the header exists.\\n\\nRequests are counted against the credential that presented them rather than the connection, over a fixed window of 60 seconds whose start the gateway doesn't disclose. Opening a second socket therefore buys a caller nothing. The count follows the credential wherever the caller puts it, including across hosts, so a client spread across eight worker processes is throttled at exactly the point one process would have been — and pays for the extra file descriptors as well.\\n\\nThe default allowance is 100 requests in a window — enough for every interactive use of this API the platform team has seen, so a caller that regularly needs more than that is almost always doing batch work under an interactive credential — and a credential marked for batch work is allowed 1000 in the same window. A refusal is not an outage, whatever the error class in a client library happens to call it: it is the platform declining to spend capacity that has already been promised to somebody else, and waiting is the only correct answer. Callers that retry at once are the largest single reason the cap is there at all.\\n\\n## How to read a refusal\\n\\nThe refusal carries a header called Retry-After. Its value is a whole number of seconds to wait, and it is present on every refusal the gateway sends. Waiting that long and then continuing is the whole of the contract — a client that does it needs nothing else from this document, which is the outcome the document was written for, however unlikely that makes it to be read at all. The gateway keeps no memory of who backed off politely, so waiting longer than the header asks earns a caller no credit at all.\\n\\nThe body of the refusal is JSON, and it names the window the gateway counted in, along with the cap and the tier. None of those fields is a substitute for the header, and a caller that parses the body to compute its own wait is doing the same arithmetic twice, in code whose only possible future is to disagree with the gateway it is talking to. The body is there so that a human reading a log afterwards can see why the request was refused — nothing in it is meant for the retry loop.\\n\\n## How to retry well\\n\\nFour rules, given in the order they should be applied.\\n\\n1. Honour the header, every time, and don't compute a wait of your own.\\n   The gateway has already done that arithmetic, with information the caller cannot see, and it holds on the 503 path too, even though the cause of that refusal is an entirely different one.\\n   A wait derived on the client side is a guess about a counter it cannot see.\\n   There is no case at all where a guess is the better of the two.\\n\\n2. Retry only what is safe to retry — a smaller set than everything that isn't a success.\\n   A 200 wants nothing, a 429 wants the stated wait, a 500 may be retried once that wait has passed, and a 503 is the overload path.\\n   The four cases are not interchangeable.\\n   A client that folds every non-success into one branch will retry hardest during exactly the incident the cap was installed to survive.\\n   Avoid writing that loop.\\n\\n3. Batch is different.\\n   A batch credential is allowed fifty times the default allowance, and it is measured over exactly the same window.\\n   The window, the header and the body are identical to the interactive case, so a client written for one tier needs no change to run against the other.\\n   The tier is set on the credential when it is issued and cannot be asked for per call.\\n   Nothing else about the two tiers differs in any way.\\n\\n4. Log every refusal seen, and keep the log for at least a week.\\n   A caller that cannot say how often it was refused last week cannot argue for a larger cap, and nobody at the far end will assemble that argument on its behalf.\\n   The log line should carry the credential, the window and the wait.\\n   The counts are the whole of the argument — without them a request is only a preference.\\n\\n## Other refusals and their codes\\n\\nNot all refusals are caps. The gateway refuses a request for three reasons and only one of them is the one above; two of the three have nothing to do with how much traffic a caller has sent in the window it is currently in. A credential may be suspended, a route may be closed for maintenance, or a body may exceed the 5 megabyte limit and be refused before it has even been read. None of those three clears itself by waiting for a stated number of seconds.\\n\\nThe distinction matters to a retry loop. A loop that waits and retries against a suspended credential spends its whole budget without ever reaching the service, and the log afterwards shows nothing at all except a long run of refusals and no successes — which reads like an outage and isn't one, and the wasted budget is the caller's own. Reading the status code rather than the class it belongs to is what separates the two cases, and it costs one comparison.\\n\\n## Asking for an increase\\n\\nA cap can be raised, and the number of caps raised without a measurement behind them is 0 — an assertion that the current one is too small is not enough on its own. Bring a week of refusal counts, the shape of the traffic across the day, and the deadline that traffic is serving. The platform team wants to know whether the load is smooth or bursty before it looks at the number at all, because a burst is cheaper to smooth than it is to serve at its peak. A caller that can move half its work to a quieter hour usually finds it doesn't need a larger cap in the first place — the cheapest fix available to anybody, and one available to almost everybody.\\n\\nRequests reach the platform team through the usual channel, and they are answered within two working days; chasing the answer doesn't make it come any faster. There is no expedited path and no exception list.\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"title\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"# What a client should do when the gateway says 429\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base document's title kept; it names the subject as fully as the alternative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"heading: retry rules section\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"## How to retry well\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"## What the client does\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"## How to retry well\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base heading kept for the section it structures.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"heading: other refusals section\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"## Other refusals and their codes\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"## Other kinds of refusal\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"## Other refusals and their codes\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base heading kept for the section it structures.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    {\"segment\": \"b1\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\", \"reason\": \"Base title chosen instead.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b2\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"Every credential has a cap.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact as a2, a's wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b3\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"When that cap is spent the gateway answers 429 at the edge\", \"reason\": \"Same fact as a3, a's fuller wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a4\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"Requests are counted against the credential that presented them rather than the connection, over a fixed window of 60 seconds whose start the gateway doesn't disclose.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b5's undisclosed-start detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b5\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"Requests are counted against the credential that presented them rather than the connection, over a fixed window of 60 seconds whose start the gateway doesn't disclose.\", \"reason\": \"Its extra detail joined with a4's counting rule.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b6\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"Opening a second socket therefore buys a caller nothing.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact as a5, a's wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a6\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The count follows the credential wherever the caller puts it, including across hosts, so a client spread across eight worker processes is throttled at exactly the point one process would have been — and pays for the extra file descriptors as well.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b7's cross-host detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b7\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The count follows the credential wherever the caller puts it, including across hosts, so a client spread across eight worker processes is throttled at exactly the point one process would have been — and pays for the extra file descriptors as well.\", \"reason\": \"Cross-host detail folded into a6's example.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b8\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"a client spread across eight worker processes is throttled at exactly the point one process would have been\", \"reason\": \"Same fact as a6, restated in combined sentence.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a7\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The default allowance is 100 requests in a window — enough for every interactive use of this API the platform team has seen, so a caller that regularly needs more than that is almost always doing batch work under an interactive credential — and a credential marked for batch work is allowed 1000 in the same window.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b9's sufficiency and misuse detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b9\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The default allowance is 100 requests in a window — enough for every interactive use of this API the platform team has seen, so a caller that regularly needs more than that is almost always doing batch work under an interactive credential — and a credential marked for batch work is allowed 1000 in the same window.\", \"reason\": \"Its added detail joined with a7's allowance figures.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b10\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"it is the platform declining to spend capacity that has already been promised to somebody else, and waiting is the only correct answer\", \"reason\": \"Same fact as a8/a9, a's wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b11\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"Callers that retry at once are the largest single reason the cap is there at all.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact as a10, a's wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b12\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"## How to read a refusal\", \"reason\": \"Identical heading text to a11.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b13\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"The refusal carries a header called Retry-After.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact as a12, wording combined into one sentence.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a13\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"Its value is a whole number of seconds to wait, and it is present on every refusal the gateway sends.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b14's every-refusal detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b14\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"Its value is a whole number of seconds to wait, and it is present on every refusal the gateway sends.\", \"reason\": \"Its added detail joined with a13's header-value rule.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a14\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"Waiting that long and then continuing is the whole of the contract — a client that does it needs nothing else from this document, which is the outcome the document was written for, however unlikely that makes it to be read at all.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b15's authorial-intent clause.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b15\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"Waiting that long and then continuing is the whole of the contract — a client that does it needs nothing else from this document, which is the outcome the document was written for, however unlikely that makes it to be read at all.\", \"reason\": \"Its added clause joined with a14's contract statement.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b16\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"so waiting longer than the header asks earns a caller no credit at all\", \"reason\": \"Same fact as a15, a's wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b17\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"The body of the refusal is JSON, and it names the window the gateway counted in, along with the cap and the tier.\", \"reason\": \"Same facts as a16/a17, combined into one sentence.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a18\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"a caller that parses the body to compute its own wait is doing the same arithmetic twice, in code whose only possible future is to disagree with the gateway it is talking to\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b18's future-disagreement framing.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b18\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"a caller that parses the body to compute its own wait is doing the same arithmetic twice, in code whose only possible future is to disagree with the gateway it is talking to\", \"reason\": \"Its framing joined with a18's redundancy point.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b19\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"The body is there so that a human